# The model-facing goal tool, driven by the model (workspace issue #259, S3).
#
# No `/goal` is typed here. The stub's "goal" mode scripts one model-initiated
# chain, and the point is what the chain proves through the real binary:
#
#   1. the model calls `goal create` (a human turn) — the goal appears, so the
#      tool's dispatcher, schema and permission path all work;
#   2. the human turn ends and the continuation driver starts round 1;
#   3. in that round the model calls `goal get`, then `goal complete` with the
#      exact {goal_id, revision} it read. The get is required: a round is a new
#      run, and the create's tool result is not in the next round's history, so
#      the stub's complete call parses the ref out of the get's result;
#   4. the goal reports complete, and the driver stops — no round 2.
#
# What it does not prove: that a real model *chooses* to create a goal from a
# sentence. The stub ignores the prompt and follows its script, so this is the
# tool path, not the decision; the build prompt's guidance is pinned by
# `agent/prompt_test.go`, and the decision itself is model behaviour.
#
# The home is its own (/tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool) and is wiped by the wrapper, so
# this scenario cannot see or leave state another one depends on.
open --dir . --size 120x40 --env HOME=/tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool --env TERM=xterm-256color --env COLORTERM=truecolor --env CLICOLOR_FORCE=1 --env NO_COLOR -- /bin/sh -c 'rm -rf /tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool && mkdir -p /tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool/.tinycode /tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool/sessions; python3 tui/testdata/stub/openai_stub.py 18523 goal 1 /tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool/count.txt & sleep 1; ./bin/tinycode --base-url=http://127.0.0.1:18523/v1 --api-key=stub --model=stub --session-dir=/tmp/tinyscen-goal-tool/sessions --log-level=error; status=$?; kill %1 2>/dev/null; exit $status'
wait --text "Type your request" --timeout 20s
# Build mode first: the session starts in plan mode, whose read-only ruleset
# denies the goal tool, so a create there would be refused (and should be — the
# docs say plan mode cannot create a goal). The assertion is the status bar's
# mode label, not the welcome menu entry.
send --text "/build"
send --key enter
wait --text "⚡ build" --timeout 15s
# "implement the whole migration" is not parsed by anyone: the stub answers with
# the create call regardless of the prompt. The assertion is about the call.
send --text "implement the whole migration"
send --key enter
# The goal exists before any /goal command: it was created through the tool.
wait --text "goal 0/256" --timeout 30s
# The human turn ends, and the driver starts round 1 on its own.
wait --text "Round: 1/256" --timeout 30s
# The round completed the goal through the tool, so the goal stopped.
wait --text "goal 1/256 done" --timeout 30s
# The goal completed inside the round, but the round's answer is still
# streaming: a command submitted now would be refused with "A run is already in
# progress". Wait for the screen to settle first (the session clock is the only
# thing still changing once the run ends, so a short stable window is enough).
stable 200ms --timeout 15s
send --text "/goal"
send --key enter
wait --text "Phase: complete" --timeout 15s
screenshot /tmp/tinyscen-shots/goal-tool-120x40.png --scale 2
# No round 2: a further round would have raised the status-bar counter. The
# counter is the only place this text appears, so it staying is the assertion.
sleep 3s
wait --text "goal 1/256 done" --timeout 10s
send --key ctrl+c --key ctrl+c
wait-exit 30s
expect-exit 0
